  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/storage.rs#impl[StoredGraph/Graph]/fn[outgoing_edges]` is appropriate.\n\n- **Form:** The citation is a fenced commentary block on an explicit function with a body. This is a permitted use of `habeas:coverage`.\n- **Substance:** The commentary gives a real reason for the zero execution of `outgoing_edges`. `Graph` requires the callback. The `StoredGraph` fixture is a storage-only consumer that must use only `node_path`. The body is a deliberate `panic!` guard that rejects any edge discovery. The test `consumer_delegates_evidence_storage_using_only_the_requested_nodes_path` calls only `load_evidence` and `store_evidence`, which use `node_path`. Running the guard would fail that test, so leaving it unexecuted is the intended result.\n- **Agreement with source:** The claim that traversal fixtures exercise edge discovery holds. Other test fixtures implement real `outgoing_edges` bodies: `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/agent.rs` (`impl[Memory/Graph]/fn[outgoing_edges]`), `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/validation.rs` (`impl[Memory/Graph]/fn[outgoing_edges]`) and the `graph` macro in `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/contracts.rs`. I did not check that those fixtures actually run during traversal. The cited reasoning rests on this claim.\n- **Scope:** The citation is not redundant. The item has no `{impl}` or `{test}` reference to the same target.",
